If the target is `es2021` or lower, `this.#x += k`, `this.#x ??= k`, and `++this.#x` on a private accessor give the wrong value. They should give the new value of `#x`, but they give the return value of the setter instead. Most setters return nothing, so the value is usually `undefined`.

```js
class Counter {
  #n = 1;
  get #count() { return this.#n; }
  set #count(v) { this.#n = v; }
  add(k) { return (this.#count += k); }
}
console.log(new Counter().add(2)); // 3
```

The transformed code prints `undefined`, because the result of the expression is the setter call:

```js
// before
return _set_count.call(_assertClassBrand(_Counter_brand, this), _get_count.call(_assertClassBrand(_Counter_brand, this)) + k);

// after
return _toSetter(_set_count.bind(_assertClassBrand(_Counter_brand, this)))._ = _get_count.call(_assertClassBrand(_Counter_brand, this)) + k;
```

Plain `=` and postfix `x++` are already correct, and plain `=` already uses `_toSetter`. This PR uses the same form if the value of the expression is used. If the expression is a statement, the output does not change.

I did not use a temp var, because a temp var in a default parameter is shared between calls, and a recursive setter overwrites it.

The new fixture `oxc/private-accessor-assignment-value` covers instance and static accessors, all the operators above, an object from a function call, arrow function bodies, and the recursive case. Its `exec.js` fails on main with `expected 'from setter' to be 3`.

